# Metal-Organic Framework Templated Synthesis of Fe2O3/TiO2 Nanocomposite for Hydrogen Production

## abstract
A new metal-organic framework (MOF)-templated method has been developed for the synthesis of a metal oxide nanocomposite with interesting photophysical properties. Fe-containing nanoscale MOFs are coated with amorphous titania, then calcined to produce crystalline Fe(2)O(3)/TiO(2) composite nanoparticles. This material enables photocatalytic hydrogen production from water using visible light, which cannot be achieved by either Fe(2)O(3) or TiO(2) alone or a mixture of the two.

Metal-Organic Framework Templated Synthesis of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}/$ $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ Nanocomposite for Hydrogen Production
Metal oxide nanomaterials are of great practical importance due to their stability, low cost, low toxicity, and useful photophysical properties. 1–4  With the ability to excite electrons to the conduction band or to generate holes in the valence band, metal oxide nanomaterials can be used to perform photocatalytic reactions, such as degradation of organic pollutants 5  or production of solar fuels like hydrogen. 6–8  Large-bandgap metal oxides such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ have suitable band positions for photocatalytic solar fuel production, but absorb only UV photons, which represent only about $5\%$ of the energy in the solar spectrum. On the other hand, metal oxides with suitable band gaps for efficient absorption in the solar spectrum (e.g., $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ tend to have short carrier diffusion lengths and are ineffective in driving photocatalytic reactions. Identifying scalable and economic approaches to synthesize metal oxide nanomaterials that not only absorb visible light $(>400\ \mathrm{nm})$ but also have suitable band positions to drive reactions is a major scientific and technological challenge. Mixed metal oxide nanocomposites offer a potential solution as a result of the synergy that is possible among the components. There have been many reports of nanocomposite materials that perform better as photocatalysts under visible light compared to their individual components. 9–14
Here we report a simple, inexpensive, tunable, and scalable metal-organic framework (MOF)-templated strategy for the synthesis of a mixed metal oxide nanocomposite with useful photophysical properties. Although MOFs have been explored for a wide range of applications such as nonlinear optics, 15 16 gas storage, 17–19  catalysis, 20 21  chemical sensing, 22–25  and drug delivery, 26–28  there have been only a few reports on using MOFs as templates in the synthesis of silica nanoshells, 29 nanoporous carbon, 30  and metallic nanoparticles. 31  In this work, we attempt to use nanoscale MOFs to prepare titaniabased nanocomposites for potential solar energy applications. Fe-containing nanoscale MOFs are coated with amorphous titania, then calcined to produce crystalline $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composite nanoparticles, which enable visible light-driven hydrogen production from water (Scheme 1).
Nanoscale MOFs with the MIL-101 32  structure and the formula $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{OCl}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ ( ${\mathrm{BDC}}=$ benzene dicarboxylate) were synthesized by microwaving a solution of benzene dicarboxylic acid $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)}$ and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $\mathrm{\DeltaN,N^{\prime}}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . 33  The resulting octahedral nanoparticles had dimensions of $400{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}$ by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Figure 1a) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Figure S4a), and were confirmed to have the MIL-101 structure by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Figure S8). MIL101 materials are extremely porous and are composed of trivalent metal clusters linked together by BDC bridging ligands.
The MIL-101 particles were coated with an amorphous shell of titania by acid-catalyzed hydrolysis and condensation of titanium(IV) bis(ammonium lactato)dihydroxide (TALH) in water. 34  The coated particles were isolated by centrifugation and washed several times with water and ethanol. The thickness of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ coating could be controlled by adjusting the concentration of acid or the reaction time (Figure S1). A typical sample used for photocatalysis was prepared by coating MIL-101 with titania for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ HCl, resulting in particles that appeared similar to the original MIL-101 particles by TEM (Figure 1b,c), but contained Ti as shown by energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) (Figure S6a). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) showed $5.6\%$ additional weight remaining at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due to the titania coating (Figure S5). Scanning TEM coupled with EDS (STEM-EDS) was used in an effort to measure the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell thickness (Figure $\mathrm{S7a}$ ), but the spatial resolution was too low to precisely delineate the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ boundaries. Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) was used to precisely determine the Fe and Ti content, after digesting the particles in concentrated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ The atomic ratio of Ti to Fe (0.25) was used to calculate the thickness of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, assuming an average MIL-101 edge length of $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ and densities of 0.62 and $3.89~\mathrm{g/cm^{3}}$ for MIL-101 and titania, respectively. The amorphous titania shell was estimated to be $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick, assuming it formed a contiguous shell on the MIL-101 particles. Although there is some loss of crystallinity indicated by PXRD, peaks from MIL-101 appear in the pattern for the coated particles, indicating that MIL-101 does not decompose under coating conditions (Figure S8). There were no additional peaks appearing in the PXRD pattern for the core-shell particles, suggesting that the titania shell was amorphous.
The core-shell particles were calcined at $550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ , resulting in a color change from orange to dark red, due to conversion of the MIL-101 core into iron oxide. The calcined particles, like the core-shell particles, are dispersible in water, indicating that the particles remain discrete and do not fuse together. PXRD showed that both the core and shell were crystallized by calcination and the PXRD peaks matched those of the anatase phase of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the hematite phase of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $(\alpha{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ (Figure 3). The three peaks that do not match either of these phases (at $17.8^{\circ}$ $45.4^{\circ}$ and $59.1^{\circ})$ are from the pseudobrookite mixed metal oxide phase $\mathrm{(Fe_{2}T i O_{5})}$ , which is probably due to the formation of an Fe-rich layer at the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ 35  The amount of Fe and Ti, as determined by
ICP-MS, corresponded to calcined particles containing $80~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $20\mathrm{\wt\%}$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ TEM shows octahedral shells with no visibly distinct $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ regions (Figure 1d). Due to the very large pore volume of MIL-101 $(2.0~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ , the non-porous ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ particles formed by calcination of this template occupy much less volume, resulting in a hollow particle. When uncoated MIL-101 particles were calcined, the resulting $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ did not remain in an octahedral structure, but formed irregular particles of ${\sim}50~\mathrm{nm}$ fused into clusters (Figure S3). This further confirms the presence of a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell that acts as a support for ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ particles as they are formed from MIL-101 during calcination. High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) was used to observe the individual crystallites of each metal oxide in the composite material. HRTEM reveals that each octahedral shell is composed of roughly spherical individual crystalline domains, with dimensions around $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ , of both $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ blended together (Figure 2). Lattice fringes are visible, and lattice d-spacings of both 0.354 and $0.251\mathrm{nm}$ were measured, corresponding to the $\{101\}$ plane of anatase $\left(\mathrm{d}\right)=$ $0.352\ \mathrm{nm}\vert$ and the $\{110\}$ plane of hematite $(\mathrm{d}=0.252\:\mathrm{nm}$ ), respectively. Figure 2b shows a crystallite of anatase, while Figure 2c shows an area with several crystalline domains with identified regions of anatase and hematite in intimate contact. A nitrogen adsorption isotherm was acquired for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}.$ resulting in a BET surface area of $11.6\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Figure S11).
Diffuse reflectance spectroscopy was used to measure the UV-vis absorbance of MIL-101, MIL ${\cdot}101@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ a physical mixture of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ (hematite) alone, and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (anatase) alone (Figure 4  Figure S10). $\mathrm{BaSO_{4}}$ was used as a blank, and absorbance was expressed by treating the reflectance data with the Kubelka-Munk function $\begin{array}{r}{(f(\mathbb{R})=}\end{array}$ $(1{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{R}})^{2}/(2\ensuremath{\mathrm{R}})$ , ${\sf R}=$ reflectance with the reflectance at $1000\ \mathrm{nm}$ set at $100\%$ ). The absorbance spectrum for MIL $-101\ @\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ showed a combination of the spectral features of MIL-101 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ alone, with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ absorbing mainly UV light while MIL101 absorption extends into the visible range. Commercially available $32~\mathrm{nm}$ anatase nanoparticles showed an absorption edge at $390\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to a band gap of $3.18\mathrm{eV}$ ( $(3.2\mathrm{eV}$ typically reported). Hematite $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles were made by calcining uncoated MIL-101, and showed an absorption edge at $585\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to a band gap of $2.12\mathrm{eV}$ $(2.1\mathrm{eV}$ typically reported). The physical mixture was prepared by combining $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ together in the same ratio as found in the composite material, giving a spectrum with a combination of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ features. While the composite material also displays the absorption edges seen for both $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ alone, it also shows increased absorption from $330{\mathrm{-}}460\ \mathrm{nm}$ . This feature is not present in the physical mixture and indicates that some of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is doped with Fe. 36 37  The bandgap of ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{5}$ is $2.2~\mathrm{eV},^{[35]}$ so any contribution at the onset of absorption from this phase would overlap with $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ absorption.
No single material has been identified that is ideal for visible light-driven water splitting. The conduction band must be at higher energy (more negative reduction potential) than the $\mathrm{H}^{+}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ couple ( $[0\mathrm{~V~}$ vs NHE), and the valence band must be at lower energy (more positive reduction potential) than the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ couple $(1.23\mathrm{V}$ vs NHE), but the band gap must be less than $3\ \mathrm{eV.}$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has been widely studied as a photocatalyst, and has suitable band positions, but absorbs only UV light. 38 $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ absorbs visible light, but its conduction band is not at high enough energy to drive $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation. 39  The photocatalytic activity of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ material in combination with $\mathrm{K_{2}P t C l_{4}}$ was tested by measuring photoinduced hydrogen production from water, with triethylamine (TEA) as a sacrificial reducing agent. A Xe-lamp was used as a light source, with a cut-off filter to allow only light above a particular wavelength to pass through. A co-catalyst such as $\mathrm{Pt}$ or $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ is typically needed to promote surface $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ formation. 40 $\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}$ was reduced to form Pt nanoparticles during the reaction, causing a change in the color of the material from dark red to brown. The presence of Pt in the material recovered after hydrogen production was confirmed by EDS and PXRD (Figure S6c and S9). HRTEM images show $2{\cdot}4\ \mathrm{nm}\ \mathrm{Pt}$ particles dispersed throughout the material (Figure 2d, Figure S2) with a lattice d-spacing of $0.228\mathrm{nm}$ , close to the $0.226\ \mathrm{nm}$ d-spacing expected for the $\{111\}$ plane of Pt. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was measured in the headspace over photodriven reactions by gas chromatography (GC). GC was performed at various time points over $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ during a reaction with $0.5\mathrm{\mg\Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in $20/1$ v/v $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{TEA}$ , driven by visible light with a $420~\mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter (Figure 5a). The amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ produced increases linearly during this entire time period, with a total of $30.0~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ per mg of material produced after $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is a heterogeneous catalyst that can be recovered from a reaction mixture and reused with a fresh solution. The catalyst produced $0.8~\mathrm{\upmumol}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ after $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ during the first use and showed no change in efficiency during the second or third use (Figure 5b). While TEM images of the recovered material show that many of the octahedral shells break apart (Figure S2), PXRD shows that the material remains unchanged with the same crystalline phases (Figure S9). In the absence of TEA, there is very little $(<0.1\ \upmu\mathrm{mol})$ ) $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ detected, indicating that a sacrifical reducing agent is necessary to balance the reduction half-reaction of water splitting (Figure 5c).
No pure component in the material is able to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ using visible light. $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ or ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{5}$ alone can not catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production because their conduction bands are below the potential for proton reduction. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ alone can not catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production because it does not absorb visible light. Reactions were carried out using similar conditions, but with $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ alone and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ alone, to confirm that neither of these materials could catalyze the reaction. As expected, in both cases, no $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was detected in the headspace after $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure 5c). A similar reaction was also carried out using a physical mixture of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ The conduction band of ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ is below that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the valence band of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is above that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ Therefore, electrons excited to the conduction band of ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ by visible light are not able to transfer to the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ conduction band and photogenerated holes are not able to transfer to the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ valence band. These energy differences prevent $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ from producing $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ using visible light energy harvested by $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and no $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was detected using a physical mixture of the two.
A reaction was carried out with $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ using a $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter to determine if Fedoped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ could be responsible for the photocatalytic activity. Fe-doping in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ raises the potential of the valence band, making the band gap of Fe-doped anatase between that of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ 37 41 42  This method of narrowing the band gap of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ by doping has been investigated extensively as a way of sensitizing $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to visible light. 35 38 43 44  Light with wavelengths higher than $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ (and lower than $590~\mathrm{nm}$ ) is absorbed by $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ but may be too low in energy to be absorbed by Fe-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ For example, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ doped with 6.7 atomic $\%$ Fe (based on Ti) is reported to have a band gap of $2.7~\mathrm{eV},^{[41]}$ which would correspond to absorbance of light with wavelength $<460\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Using similar conditions as the reaction that produces $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with a $420~\mathrm{nm}$ filter, no $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is detected with a $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ filter (Figure 5c), consistent with Fe-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as the catalytic component of the composite material (Scheme S1). Apparently, during calcination, some $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ from MIL-101 is incorporated into $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as it crystallizes while some turns into $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ Both ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{5}$ and Ti-doped ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ were ruled out as potential photocatalytically active components because their conduction bands are below the potential for proton reduction, and their small bandgaps (2.2 and $2.1\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively) would allow light absorption when a $500\mathrm{nm}$ filter is used. 41
In conclusion, we have developed a novel MOFtemplated approach to synthesize a mixed metal oxide nanocomposite material by first creating core-shell particles, then calcining to decompose the core. This facile method produces crystalline octahedral nanoshells composed of hematite $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles embedded in anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with some Fe doping. This material has interesting photophysical properties as it enables photocatalytic hydrogen production from water using visible light, while neither component alone is able to do so. The versatile MOF-templated nanocomposite synthesis procedure can be readily modified, by varying the type of MOF (both the metal ions and coordinating moieties) and the coating material, to prepare new nanocomposite materials with desirable synergistic properties.
## Experimental Section
Synthesis of MIL-101: Benzene dicarboxylic acid $(1.532~\mathrm{g},9.22~\mathsf{m m o l})$ and $\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $(2.492\ \mathrm{g},9.22\ \mathsf{m m o l})$ were dissolved in DMF $(200~\mathrm{mL})$ . This solution was heated by microwave in sealed vessels at $400~\mathsf{W}$ for $15\mathrm{\min}$ at $\mathsf{l}50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The resulting particles were isolated by centrifugation at $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\boldmath~\l~}}_{1}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\boldmath~\oo0~}}$ rpm for $10\min$ , washed with ethanol and water several times by sonication followed by centrifugation, and then dispersed in water. Yield: $543.7\mathrm{mg}$ $(94.7\%)$ .
Synthesis of $M I L-707@T i O_{2}$ and $F e_{2}O_{3}@T i O_{2}.$ MIL-101 $(200~\mathsf{m g})$ was dispersed in 0.1 M HCl $(96.725~\mathsf{m L})$ , and then TALH $(3.275~\mathsf{m L}$ of $50\mathrm{\textmu}\mathrm{wt}\%$ solution in water, $2.00\textrm{g T A L H}$ was added. The dispersion was stirred at room temperature for $2h$ . The resulting coated particles were isolated by centrifugation as described above, and dried. Yield: $\mathsf{140.8~m g}$ $(70.4\%$ based on MIL-101 used). Dried $\mathsf{M l L}.70\rceil\ @\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was calcined in a furnace at $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in air for $\mathsf{16h}$ .
Procedure for hydrogen production experiments: Samples for photoinduced hydrogen production were prepared in $8m L$ septumsealed glass vials. Typically, $0.5~\mathsf{m g}$ of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}@\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ $(5.0~\upmu\mathsf{m}\circ|$ Fe, $1.25~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ Ti) was dispersed in $2m L$ of $H_{2}O$ with $0.1~\mathsf{m L}$ of TEA, and ${\sf K}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{4}$ $(3.70~\upmu\mathrm{mol})$ was added. The sample vials were capped and deoxygenated by bubbling nitrogen through them for 15 min. $50~\upmu\up L$ of methane gas (1 atm, room temperature) was added to each vial as an internal standard. The vials were placed in front of a 450 W Xe-lamp with a $420~\mathsf{n m}$ cut-off filter (unless otherwise noted) and were stirred magnetically. The hydrogen evolution reaction lasted for 3 hours except for the time-dependent experiments. $0.5~\mathsf{m L}$ of the gas in the headspace of the vial was analyzed by GC to determine the amount of hydrogen generated.
